in this episode we're going to build  toast style notification messages from  scratch the messages will be saved on  the backend and firebase as well so we  can maintain a cool history of the  messages that have been sent the  messages are managed by a central  service that can be injected into any  component this makes it possible to  trigger messages from anywhere in the  app on the front-end we're using the  boma CSS framework but you could easily  swap this out for bootstrap and material  or your own custom classes let's start  by building the toast service we'll  first start by importing the angularfire  database then we'll create a custom  message class just to model our data a  message can app content is a string a  style and it can also be dismissed which  is a boolean value in the constructor we  send at the content and an optional  style argument if the style is undefined  it'll just default to PIMCO in the  service we create a function to return  the firebase list observable with the  messages in the database we want to  limit this query to the last five  messages from there we create a function  to create a new message this function  can be called anywhere from any  component and it will update the  firebase was too observable it simply  takes the content and style as arguments  and builds a new message and sends that  to the database and the last step is to  give the user the ability to dismiss  messages so we just retrieve a message  as an object and then update it if  missed attribute to true before we start  building the component I'm going to  build a custom pipe that will filter out  the dismissed messages and then send  them in reversed order so they display  one on top of the other first I import  the filter and reverse functions from  lodash and the value going into this  pipe will be an array of messages so we  can call reverse and then we can filter  out that dismiss messages and if there's  no value we just return null  now we can put the pipe to use in the  component template the messages will be  in the form of fire based lists  observable which we can unwrap with the  async pipe we can then chain our custom  reverse pipe to that to send them in  reverse order and filtered by the  dismissed attribute from there we can  use ng class to display a conditional  class based on the style of the message  we passed ng class an object where the  key is the Bulma CSS class and the value  is true or false based on how the  statement evaluates the final step is to  allow the user to dismiss the message by  clicking a button and we also display  the message content going into the type  script we first inject the toast service  into the component and then we get a  list of messages as the firebase lists  observable then we set up an event  handler to handle the click of the  button to dismiss the message in this  example I want toast messages to be  global throughout the application so I  put them in the app component outside  the scope of the router so they'll still  display even if the user navigates to  different pages now we can inject the  service and any component we want and in  this case we'll just send a few  arbitrary messages from the readme page  component if we go back to the app we  can see the database is being updated  after each button click you may or may  not want to save these messages in the  database but when you do it opens up  some possibilities to do things with  cloud functions where you could update  something in the database and then push  a message right into the user speed  [Music]  that's it for this episode if you found  the video helpful please like and  subscribe and if you want to support the  channel consider becoming a pro  subscriber at angular firebase comm for  just a few bucks a month you'll get  access to you exclusive content as well  as free one on one project consulting  thanks for watching and I'll see you  next time  [Music]
 

